his mechanism boundary: Reflexion control flow and state isolation; base-agent and evaluator model calls are supplied as deterministic fixtures.\" The attempts array contains the results of previous attempts. Each attempt has success, failure_signature (maybe only if failure?), reflection. So we are given these. We need to decide whether to continue or stop, and produce memory_append (which is the reflection and signature from the current attempt? Or the next one? The spec says \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature\". That likely means after each attempt (including the last one), we append that attempt's reflection and signature to memory. But the output contract's memory_append is a single object (or null) to be appended. It should be the reflection and signature from the current (last) attempt? But note: the function takes a case with attempts list. That list includes all previous attempts. So the function must analyze the last attempt (if any) and decide next action. If we are to continue, we need to set next_attempt to the next attempt number (len(attempts)+1?). If we stop, we set decision \"stop\" and next_attempt should be? The spec: \"return decision, next index, and memory update.\" The next index likely is the attempt number that was just completed? Or the next attempt number? For stop, maybe next_attempt should be the current attempt number? But the output schema has next_attempt as integer. Probably it's the next attempt number to be used (if continuing) or the current attempt number (if stopping) as a record. Let's infer from the fixtures.\n\nFixtures:\n- Case 0: attempts=[{success:true, failure_signature:\"e0\", reflection:\"adjust-0\"}], max_attempts=4. Since success true, we should stop. The decision \"stop\", memory_append should be the reflection and signature from that attempt (reflection:\"adjust-0\", signature:\"e0\"? but signature? The failure_signature is given but for success it's still \"e0\"? Possibly irrelevant). next_attempt: should be 1? or 2? The last attempt index is 1? Actually attempt numbers likely start at 1. Attempts list index 0 is attempt 1. So after success, we might set next_attempt to 1? Or to 2? Typically after success, we stop, so next_attempt maybe 1 (the attempt number that succeeded). But spec says \"return decision, next index, and memory update.\" It might be the index of the next attempt to try (if continuing) or the index of the current attempt (if stopping). However, the output contract says next_attempt type integer, required. We need to determine.\n\nConsider case 1: attempts=[{success:false, ...}, {success:false, ...}, {success:false, ...}], max_attempts=4. Three failures, no repeated signature? signatures: e0, e1, e2 - all distinct. Budget not exhausted (3 attempts used, max=4). So we should continue. decision \"continue\", next_attempt should be 4 (next attempt). memory_append: should be the reflection of the last attempt? That would be \"adjust-2\" with signature \"e2\". So memory_append object with reflection:\"adjust-2\", signature:\"e2\".\n\nCase 2: attempts=[... four failures], max_attempts=4. Fourth attempt failed, budget exhausted. Stop. Also check repeated failure signature? The signatures: e0, e1, e2, e0 - note e0 repeated (first and last). That is a repeated failure signature. So stop due to repeated signature as well. Decision \"stop\". next_attempt should be? Probably 4 (the last attempt number). memory_append: reflection of last attempt \"adjust-3\", signature \"e0\".\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n- Let attempts = case['attempts'] (list)\n- Let max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\n- Determine if attempts is empty: then no prior attempt, set decision \"continue\", next_attempt = 1, memory_append = None? But spec says \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature\". For first attempt, there's no reflection yet. So memory_append should be null? The schema allows null. So we can set memory_append = None.\n- If attempts non-empty:\n  - Let last_attempt = attempts[-1]\n  - Let current_attempt_number = len(attempts)  (since 1-indexed)\n  - Let reflection = last_attempt.get('reflection', '')\n  - Let signature = last_attempt.get('failure_signature', '')  # Even if success, it's present? In fixtures it's present. Use that.\n  - Determine if stop condition:\n      a. Success: last_attempt['success'] == True -> stop\n      b. Budget exhausted: current_attempt_number >= max_attempts -> stop\n      c. Repeated failure signature: check if signature is not empty and appears in any previous attempt (excluding last). But careful: repeated failure signature means same signature as any earlier failure? Or same as any earlier attempt? The spec: \"Stop on ... a repeated failure signature.\" Likely if the failure signature of the last attempt matches any previous failure's signature (including possibly success? but success may have a signature too). In fixtures, success also has failure_signature. But presumably repeated failure signature only matters for failures? But the spec says \"repeated failure signature\". If last attempt is success, we stop anyway, so no need. For failures, we check if this signature has been seen before in any earlier attempt? The spec says \"a repeated failure signature\" i.e., the same failure signature occurs again. So if the failure signature of the current attempt matches any previous attempt's failure_signature (regardless of success?), then stop. In case 2, e0 appears first and fourth. So stop.\n  - If any stop condition true:\n      decision = \"stop\"\n      next_attempt = current_attempt_number  # or maybe the number? Since we stop, we don't need next. Could be the current attempt number as a record.\n      memory_append = {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature}\n  - Else (continue):\n      decision = \"continue\"\n      next_attempt = current_attempt_number + 1\n      memory_append = {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature}\n\nBut what about the case where attempts is empty? Then no last attempt, so no reflection. memory_append should be null. decision \"continue\", next_attempt=1.\n\nEdge: if attempts non-empty but last attempt is success, we reflect it and stop. That seems fine.\n\nNow, validation: \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" The payload has attempts and max_attempts. attempts is a list. We should check that each attempt has success, failure_signature, reflection. But we can assume from fixtures.\n\nAlso note that the output schema requires \"decision\", \"memory_append\", \"next_attempt\" all present. We must return exactly that.\n\nImplementation: use standard Python, no imports beyond standard library. We'll define solve(case) that returns a dict.\n\nMake sure to not mutate case.\n\nWe'll also handle case where attempts is empty.\n\nNow, produce the Python source as a JSON string inside {\"implementation\": \"...\"}.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nPotential nuance: For empty attempts, do we set next_attempt=1? That matches \"request attempt one\". Also memory_append=null. Decision \"continue\".\n\nFor non-empty, if last attempt success, stop. But what about budget exhausted? If last attempt success and also budget exhausted? Then success overrides? The spec says \"Stop immediately after success.\" So we stop regardless of budget. So check success first.\n\nFor repeated failure signature: we need to check if the signature of the last attempt (if not success) matches any earlier attempt's failure_signature. But if last attempt is success, we stop anyway. So for failures, check if signature appears in first len(attempts)-1 attempts' failure_signature. Note that signature could be empty? Not given.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    attempts = case['attempts']\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\n    if not attempts:\n        return {\n            \"decision\": \"continue\",\n            \"memory_append\": None,\n            \"next_attempt\": 1\n        }\n    last = attempts[-1]\n    reflection = last['reflection']\n    signature = last['failure_signature']\n    current_num = len(attempts)\n    # check success\n    if last['success']:\n        return {\n            \"decision\": \"stop\",\n            \"memory_append\": {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature},\n            \"next_attempt\": current_num  # or 1? but usually the attempt number\n        }\n    # check budget\n    if current_num >= max_attempts:\n        return {\n            \"decision\": \"stop\",\n            \"memory_append\": {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature},\n            \"next_attempt\": current_num\n        }\n    # check repeated failure signature\n    # check if any earlier attempt has same failure_signature\n    for a in attempts[:-1]:\n        if a['failure_signature'] == signature:\n            return {\n                \"decision\": \"stop\",\n                \"memory_append\": {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature},\n                \"next_attempt\": current_num\n            }\n    # else continue\n    return {\n        \"decision\": \"continue\",\n        \"memory_append\": {\"reflection\": reflection, \"signature\": signature},\n        \"next_attempt\": current_num + 1\n    }\n\nThat should work for the fixtures.\n\nBut we need to ensure that for empty attempts, memory_append is null, not an empty object.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as a string containing the Python source. Remember to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's write the source with proper indentation. Use triple-quoted string? Actually we need to embed as a JSON string. We'll write a Python source with 4 spaces. Then we need to encode it as a JSON string: backslashes become \\\\, newlines become \\n, quotes become \\\". But simpler: we can use json.dumps to do that. But we are writing by hand. We'll produce the JSON manually.\n\nI'll write:\n\n{\n  \"im